course: medicine
lesson: 28
title: The hospital ward, operations and anaesthetic
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A children's ward is built for children: there are nurses who check you day and night, play specialists, sometimes a hospital school, and a bed or chair for a parent to stay beside you.
explain: Before an operation the anaesthetist, a doctor whose whole job is keeping you safe asleep, gives you a general anaesthetic through a mask or a thin tube in your hand.
explain: It isn't ordinary sleep: you feel nothing and remember nothing, and the anaesthetist watches your heart, breathing and oxygen the whole time, then you wake up in recovery.
explain: The rules that seem odd have good reasons: no food beforehand so nothing can come up into the lungs, numbing cream so the needle hurts less, and a form signed so everyone agrees what will happen.
fact: Children's wards have nurses, doctors and play specialists, and usually a bed or chair so a parent can stay overnight.
fact: Nurses check temperature, pulse and breathing regularly, even at night, and being woken for checks doesn't mean something is wrong.
fact: Before a general anaesthetic the stomach must be empty, called nil by mouth, so food can't come up and get into the lungs; if someone eats anyway, the operation may have to be put off.
fact: An anaesthetist gives the anaesthetic by mask or through a cannula, a thin plastic tube in a vein, and watches the patient throughout the operation.
fact: Numbing cream put on about an hour before makes the skin numb so a needle or cannula hurts less.
fact: As the anaesthetic wears off, children may feel sleepy, sick or muddled, and recovery nurses give medicine for sickness or pain.
fact: Keyhole surgery uses small cuts and a camera, so healing is usually quicker and scars smaller; appendix operations are often done this way.
fact: Play specialists use dolls, pictures and games to show children what will happen and to distract them during procedures.
fact: Hospital schools teach children who are in hospital for a long time so they don't fall behind.
fact: A drip gives fluids or medicines straight into a vein, and its pump beeps when it needs attention, not usually because of danger.
fact: Before an operation doctors explain it, its risks and benefits, and a parent signs a consent form; children are asked to agree too and may ask any question.
fact: On the morning ward round doctors review each patient and plan the day's treatment, and lots of doctors at the bed is normal.
exercise: Who can usually stay with a child overnight on a children's ward? | A parent | Nobody at all, visitors go home | Only the teacher from school | Only the doctor on duty
exercise: Why do nurses wake you in the night? | To check temperature, pulse and breathing | Because something has gone badly wrong with you | To give you breakfast | To change the sheets on the bed every hour
exercise: Why mustn't you eat before an operation? | Food could come up into the lungs under anaesthetic | Food makes the operation hurt | The surgeon is hungry | Food stops the cream working
exercise: What is the rule called? | Nil by mouth | Empty tummy rule | Lights out | Clean hands
exercise: Who puts you to sleep for an operation? | The anaesthetist | The surgeon who does the cutting | The play specialist | The porter
exercise: Is a general anaesthetic like normal sleep? | No, you feel and remember nothing and are watched all the time | Yes, it is exactly the same as going to sleep in your bed | Yes, but you might wake up in the middle and feel it all | No, you stay awake the whole time but your body is frozen quite still
exercise: What does numbing cream do? | Makes the skin numb so the needle hurts less | Sends you gently off to sleep before the operation | Cleans all the germs off the skin | Makes the veins hide so the needle can't find them
exercise: How long before the needle is the cream usually put on? | About an hour | About a second before | About a week before | About a whole day
exercise: How might you feel waking up after an anaesthetic? | Sleepy, sick or muddled for a while | Exactly as normal, straight away | Super strong and full of fizzing energy | Unable to speak for about a week
exercise: What is keyhole surgery? | Surgery through small cuts using a camera | Surgery done through a real keyhole in a door | Surgery done by a robot without any doctors | Surgery on locks and keys
exercise: Why is keyhole surgery often better? | Quicker healing and smaller scars | It's done while you are wide awake | It needs no anaesthetic at all | It can be done at home on the sofa
exercise: What does a play specialist do? | Shows and explains treatments through play | Plays football with the doctors at lunchtime | Cooks the hospital meals for children | Gives the anaesthetic before an operation
exercise: Why is there a school in some hospitals? | So children in for a long time don't fall behind | To punish ill children | For nurses' training only | To keep visitors busy
exercise: What does a drip do? | Gives fluids or medicines straight into a vein | Measures how tall you have grown each day in bed | Collects tears when you cry in bed | Takes X-ray pictures of your bones
exercise: Why does the drip pump beep? | It needs attention, usually not danger | The patient is very ill and might die | It is broken forever and must be thrown out | It plays a tune to help you sleep
exercise: What is the consent form for? | Agreeing to the operation after it's explained | Paying the hospital for the operation and the bed | Choosing your meals for the week | Booking a holiday for when you get better
exercise: What happens on the ward round? | Doctors review each patient and plan the day | Nurses race round the ward | Visitors take turns | Patients swap beds
test: "Sneak a biscuit before your op, nobody'll know" — what could happen? | It's dangerous and the operation may be cancelled | Nothing, a small biscuit doesn't count as food | It makes the anaesthetic work stronger and faster | You'll heal faster with some sugar inside
test: Ruby fears waking up in the middle of her operation. Best answer? | The anaesthetist watches her the whole time to keep her asleep | She will, but only briefly | Everyone wakes up halfway | She must stay awake anyway
test: Five doctors stand round your bed in the morning. Means? | It's the normal ward round | You're very ill and about to die | You've done something wrong and are in trouble | They're lost and looking for the way out
test: In a cartoon a surgeon knocks a patient out with a mallet. What's real? | An anaesthetist uses medicine and watches the patient | Surgeons really do that, but with a soft rubber hammer | Patients just hold their breath until they faint | They sing a lullaby until the patient drops off
test: Your drip beeps all night and you panic. What's true? | It usually means it needs a nurse to check it | It's a danger alarm and you must shout for help | You must pull it out yourself straight away | It means the medicine is going in the wrong way
test: Josh had keyhole surgery for his appendix and says "it was a tiny op". Fair? | The cuts were small but the work inside was a real operation | Yes, they did almost nothing | Yes, it was done through his mouth | No, keyhole means no cuts
test: What would happen without play specialists? | Children might be more scared and understand less | Operations would be faster and much cheaper for everyone | Nurses would be happier with less to do | Nothing at all, they only play games
test: "can I ask questions before I sign or is that rude?" | You may ask anything before agreeing | It's rude, just sign and trust the doctor | Only grown-ups are allowed to ask questions | Ask after the op, when it's all over
test: Kayla is in hospital for 3 months. Will she miss all her school? | No, a hospital school can teach her | Yes, there's no school in any hospital | Yes, school is banned in hospital to let children rest | Only if she asks the doctor to stop it
test: You wake from the op feeling sick and confused. What now? | Tell the recovery nurse, it's normal and can be treated | Get up quickly and walk home to your own bed | Hide it from everyone so you're let out of hospital sooner | Eat a big meal fast to settle your stomach
test: Why is the numbing cream put on an hour before the cannula, not just before? | The cream needs time to numb the skin | The cream only works on cold skin | The cannula must go in first to guide it | The cream is really for healing the hole afterwards
exam: Identify the principal reason for which patients are required to abstain from food prior to a general anaesthetic. | To prevent the contents of the stomach from entering the lungs | To reduce the cost of hospital catering | To render the anaesthetic more palatable | To accelerate the healing of the wound
exam: Ascertain the medical practitioner responsible for the administration of a general anaesthetic and the monitoring of the patient throughout the operation. | The anaesthetist | The surgeon | The play specialist | The radiographer in the X-ray department
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning keyhole surgery. | It is performed through small incisions with a camera and ordinarily permits swifter healing | It is performed without incision of any kind, the instruments entering through the natural openings of the body | It is performed only upon adults | It is performed without anaesthesia in every instance
exam: Whereas the infusion pump of an intravenous drip emits an audible alarm, determine the most accurate characterisation of such alarm. | A signal that attention is required, not ordinarily indicative of danger | A certain indication of grave danger to the life of the patient | A signal that the operation is about to commence in the theatre | A malfunction requiring the patient to remove the drip without delay
